    "excerpt": "Use Loom website audits to personalize agency outreach, structure prospecting videos, save research time, and create stronger calls to action.",
    "body": "If you are trying to use Loom website audits in agency outreach, the answer is not one tactic. The strongest web design agencies combine focused targeting, useful prospect research, clear business evidence, disciplined follow-up, and a sales process that makes the need for a better website easier to understand.\n\n## Why video works well for website selling\n\nA website is visual, which makes screen-recorded outreach a natural format. You can show the prospect exactly what you mean while they see their own site on screen. Video also gives the message a human face and makes it easier to demonstrate nuance than a long cold email.\n\nLoom itself positions personalized video as a sales use case for prospect engagement, pitches, follow-up, and calls to action. The opportunity for web agencies is to combine that medium with better pre-call research.\n\n## A simple five-minute Loom structure\n\nKeep the video focused. Open with who you are and why you looked at the site. Show one positive element so the review does not feel like an attack. Walk through two or three high-priority findings. Translate each one into a customer or business consequence. End with a specific invitation: review the full assessment, book a call, or reply if improving the site is on the roadmap.\n\nYou do not need to audit the entire website live. In fact, live wandering often produces long, unfocused videos.\n\n## What to include in a website audit Loom\n\nPrioritize findings that are easy to see on screen: unclear calls to action, weak mobile layouts, missing proof, confusing navigation, outdated visuals, thin service content, contact friction, or a mismatch between ads/search intent and landing pages. Mention technical or SEO issues only when you can explain them simply.\n\nUse the prospect’s language. If they call a job an ‘estimate,’ do not repeatedly call it a ‘lead conversion event.’\n\n## How long should the Loom be?\n\nFor cold outreach, shorter is usually easier to consume. Aim to earn the next step rather than deliver a complete consultation. For a warm lead or scheduled follow-up, a longer walkthrough can make sense because the prospect has already given you attention.\n\nThe right length is the shortest video that establishes credibility, shows meaningful evidence, and makes the next action obvious.\n\n## How to make Loom outreach faster\n\nSeparate research from recording. Use a repeatable pre-recording checklist or automated assessment to identify the strongest findings first. Then record from a prepared outline. This reduces pauses, tab switching, and rambling.\n\nPitchProof can function as the research and structure layer: scan the website first, choose the best findings, and use the report as your Loom outline.\n\n## How to scale personalized video without losing personalization\n\nStandardize the format, not the evidence. Keep the same opening structure, visual order, and CTA, but change the findings and business interpretation for each prospect. A library of niche-specific examples can also speed up explanation without making the video generic.\n\nLoom offers sales-oriented personalization and engagement features, but your competitive advantage still comes from what you notice about the prospect and how well you connect it to their goals.\n\n## Where PitchProof fits\n\nPitchProof is a sales-enablement platform for agencies that sell websites. It analyzes a prospect’s current website and packages the findings into a client-friendly report so the agency can explain what matters, why it matters, and what should be improved. Use it as the evidence layer behind your outreach and sales process rather than as a replacement for specialist SEO or developer tools.\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n### How do I use Loom to get web design clients?\nUse a repeatable process: narrow the target, research the prospect, identify specific website opportunities, translate them into business consequences, make a low-friction offer, and follow up with evidence rather than generic reminders.\n\n### How do I create a Loom website audit?\nUse a repeatable process: narrow the target, research the prospect, identify specific website opportunities, translate them into business consequences, make a low-friction offer, and follow up with evidence rather than generic reminders.\n\n### What should I include in a website audit Loom?\nUsually, if it helps the prospect understand the business case without adding friction. Keep the material concise, relevant to the buyer, and tied to the next step in the sales process.\n\n### Do personalized Loom videos work for agency outreach?\nYes, when the tactic is personalized and connected to a real business problem. The quality of the research and relevance of the message matter more than the format alone.\n\n### How do I make Loom outreach faster?\nUse a repeatable process: narrow the target, research the prospect, identify specific website opportunities, translate them into business consequences, make a low-friction offer, and follow up with evidence rather than generic reminders.\n\n### How do I scale personalized Loom audits?\nUse a repeatable process: narrow the target, research the prospect, identify specific website opportunities, translate them into business consequences, make a low-friction offer, and follow up with evidence rather than generic reminders.\n\n### How long should a website audit Loom be?\nYes, when the tactic is personalized and connected to a real business problem. The quality of the research and relevance of the message matter more than the format alone.\n\n### What should I say in a web design prospecting Loom?\nUsually, if it helps the prospect understand the business case without adding friction. Keep the material concise, relevant to the buyer, and tied to the next step in the sales process.\n\n### How do I structure a website audit video?\nUse a repeatable process: narrow the target, research the prospect, identify specific website opportunities, translate them into business consequences, make a low-friction offer, and follow up with evidence rather than generic reminders.\n\n### Is there a faster alternative to manually auditing websites for Loom outreach?\nYes, when the tactic is personalized and connected to a real business problem. The quality of the research and relevance of the message matter more than the format alone.",
